## ASSEMBLED SECTION



### <img src="https://flagcdn.com/sa.svg" width="32" alt="Saudi Arabia" style={{display: 'inline', verticalAlign: 'middle', position: 'relative', top: '-2px', marginRight: '8px', border: '1px solid #d1d5db', borderRadius: '2px'}} />Saudi Arabia

Saudi Arabia operates as a hyper-centralized petrostate under Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman, who has consolidated unprecedented personal authority while executing the most significant adjustment of Vision 2030 ambitions to date. The kingdom demonstrates sophisticated triangulation strategy, systematically providing private encouragement to US action against Iran while publicly assuring Iran of neutrality. NEOM's dramatic scaling back—Line reduced 97%, $8 billion PIF write-down—represents the most concrete evidence of fiscal reality constraining megaproject ambitions, while PIF asset growth to $930 billion shows continued sovereign wealth expansion within realistic parameters. Growing Saudi-UAE strategic divergence represents fundamental shift in Gulf alliance architecture. Critical vulnerabilities persist in geographic concentration of oil infrastructure, dependence on desalinated water, and social contract renegotiation dependent on continuous job creation.

**Key developments:**

- **Diplomatic:** Prince Khalid bin Salman reportedly told US officials that failing to follow through on threats against Iran would strengthen Tehran's regime, while MBS separately assured Iranian President Pezeshkian that Saudi Arabia will not allow its territory or airspace to be used for military actions against Iran
- **Economic:** Mukaab cube-shaped skyscraper construction suspended while project reassesses financing and feasibility. The Line reduced from 170km to approximately 1.5 miles. PIF took $8 billion write-down. New contracts collapsed from $16.2 billion in 2023 to under $900 million in Q1 2025
- **Economic:** Public Investment Fund achieved $930 billion in assets under management, positioning it among world's five largest sovereign funds, while approaching wealthy Saudi families for increased domestic investment
- **Security:** State Department approved potential $9 billion FMS for 730 PAC-3 missiles and related equipment to enhance Saudi integrated air and missile defense capabilities
- **Domestic:** London High Court ruled Saudi Arabia responsible for Pegasus spyware infection and 2018 physical attack on satirist Ghanem al-Masarir, ordering £3,025,663 in compensation for what judge called effectively criminal conduct

<Accordion title="Other Stories">
- **Saudi Arabia drastically scales back NEOM megaproject, pivots toward data centers** — Saudi Arabia is significantly downsizing its flagship NEOM megaproject after years of cost overruns and delays. The Line will be reduced from 170km to a much smaller development, and the project may pivot to becoming a data center hub rather than a futuristic city. The 2029 Asian Winter Games at Trojena have been postponed indefinitely. *([ft.com](https://www.ft.com/content/036b158f-ea34-46d1-ad0a-093eba031501))*
- **Saudi Defense Minister tells US officials not striking Iran would 'embolden' regime** — Saudi Defense Minister Prince Khalid bin Salman, during his Washington visit, reportedly told US officials that failing to follow through on threats against Iran would strengthen Tehran's regime. This marks a shift from earlier Saudi public positions urging caution against escalation. *([axios.com](https://www.axios.com/2026/01/31/saudi-us-strike-iran-kbs-trump))*
- **US approves $9 billion Patriot missile sale to Saudi Arabia** — The US State Department approved a potential $9 billion foreign military sale of Patriot Advanced Capability-3 missiles to Saudi Arabia. The deal includes 730 PAC-3 missiles and related equipment, part of broader US arms sales to Middle East allies. *([dsca.mil](https://dsca.mil/Press-Media/Major-Arms-Sales/Article-Display/Article/4394629/kingdom-of-saudi-arabia-patriot-advanced-capability-3-missile-segment-enhanceme))*
- **Saudi Crown Prince tells Iran that Kingdom won't allow US use of airspace against Tehran** — Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman told Iranian President Masoud Pezeshkian in a phone call that Saudi Arabia will not allow its airspace or territory to be used for military actions against Iran, as the US considers potential strikes. *([reuters.com](https://reuters.com/world/middle-east/iran-welcomes-any-process-prevent-war-president-tells-saudi-crown-prince-phone-2026-01-27))*
- **Saudi Aramco issues $4 billion bond in first global debt market foray of 2026** — Saudi Aramco launched a $4 billion four-tranche bond offering with maturities ranging from three to 30 years, drawing robust investor demand in the state oil company's first international bond issue this year. *([reuters.com](https://reuters.com/business/energy/saudi-aramco-taps-global-debt-markets-with-4-billion-bond-issue-2026-01-27))*
- **Saudi PIF ends year-long PwC consultancy ban after hiring dispute** — Saudi Arabia's Public Investment Fund lifted its ban on new advisory contracts with PwC after a year-long freeze. The dispute arose when PwC tried to hire the chief internal audit officer from NEOM, prompting PIF to restrict new consulting work. *([semafor.com](https://www.semafor.com/article/01/26/2026/saudi-pif-ends-pwc-consultancy-ban))*
- **Saudi Foreign Minister conducts diplomatic meetings with Austria and Poland** — Prince Faisal bin Farhan held meetings with his Austrian and Polish counterparts, reviewing bilateral relations and cooperation opportunities. He also commented on Saudi-UAE relations, calling them 'critically important' for regional stability despite differences over Yemen. *([english.aawsat.com](https://english.aawsat.com/gulf/5235155-saudi-foreign-minister-meets-austrian-counterpart))*
- **OPEC+ maintains oil production levels unchanged for March** — Eight OPEC+ countries agreed to keep oil production cuts in place for March, maintaining current output levels despite oil prices reaching six-month highs. The decision comes amid concerns about potential US military action against Iran affecting markets. *([reuters.com](https://reuters.com/ar/business/LUXLHO2APNIH7CNWF4QHJBDBJY-2026-02-01))*
- **Analysis: Saudi-UAE divergence grows amid regional competition** — Multiple analytical pieces examine the growing strategic divergence between Saudi Arabia and the UAE, with Saudi Arabia aligning more with Turkey and Qatar while the UAE strengthens ties with Israel and the US. The rift is playing out in proxy conflicts across Yemen, Sudan, and Libya. *([jpost.com](https://warontherocks.com/2026/01/risk-order-and-power-the-saudi-emirati-divergence))*
</Accordion>

